INT. COMMUNITY CENTER BASEMENT - FOLDING-CHAIR CIRCLE - NIGHT

A low-ceilinged room with a linoleum floor and a coffee urn that has been warm since 1994. Posters of tide pools on cinderblock walls. Five chairs in a circle, one empty.

FACILITATOR DOLORES "DOLLIE" ENGSTROM-ACHEBE (60s, cardigan, kind eyes, a voice trained to be even) sits with a clipboard on her knee.

CORNELL BEAUMONT-ASANTE (40s, mechanic's hands scrubbed pink, work shirt with his name stitched on) grips a paper cup.

YASMINE KOWALSKI-HADDAD (30s, scrubs, hair coming loose, eyes bright with no sleep) sits with her feet tucked under her.

OLD MR. TIBERIUS FAIRWEATHER-MWANGI (80s, tweed cap in his lap, hearing aid whistling softly) leans on a cane.

HARPER DELACROIX-YAMAMOTO (17, hoodie, earbuds around neck) has chosen the chair nearest the door.

DOLLIE
Thank you for coming. Who would like to start?

CORNELL
I'll start.

DOLLIE
Thank you, Cornell.

CORNELL
My wife used to say I never talked. Said I could run a whole day on three words. Truth is I just never knew where to put the rest of them.

YASMINE
That's me with my mother. Eleven years of silence in the same kitchen.

CORNELL
And now she's gone.

YASMINE
And now the kitchen's loud.

TIBERIUS
What's that?

DOLLIE
She says the kitchen's loud, Tiberius.

TIBERIUS
Mine's the opposite. Mine hums. The refrigerator hums so much I think it's her.

HARPER
That's not weird.

TIBERIUS
It's a bit weird, young man.

HARPER
I'm not a man.

TIBERIUS
Young person, then. Forgive me.

HARPER
It's fine.

DOLLIE
Harper, would you like to share?

HARPER
No.

DOLLIE
That's all right.

HARPER
Okay, maybe.

A long pause. The urn gurgles.

HARPER (CONT'D)
My dad's phone still gets texts. I read them sometimes. People tell him about their day. I don't know why I keep reading.

YASMINE
You keep him company.

HARPER
He's not there.

YASMINE
Neither's my mother, and I still set two cups.

CORNELL
Two cups.

YASMINE
Every morning.

CORNELL
I do that with the truck. I leave the passenger seat clear.

TIBERIUS
What for?

CORNELL
In case.

TIBERIUS
Ah.

DOLLIE
It's a good thing, to leave room.

CORNELL
Is it?

DOLLIE
I think so. I leave a chair in this circle every week. For whoever isn't here yet.

HARPER
That's why there's an empty one.

DOLLIE
That's why.

TIBERIUS
It's not empty. My Winnie sits there.

Silence. Nobody laughs.

CORNELL
(low)
Mine too.

YASMINE
Mine's in the kitchen.

HARPER
(very quietly)
Mine's on the phone.

DOLLIE
Then we're full tonight.
